#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134760} :activity/id #uuid "e1b56295-70ee-55b2-8a65-97f8ae733716" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048308 :topic/id #uuid "93699c29-7fa0-5489-8437-1976b79a70b4" :topic/math-academy-id 2110} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134760 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135084 :tutorial/content "Suppose that $f(x)$ is continuous on $[a,b].$ The fundamental theorem of calculus states that\n\n$$\n\\int_a^b f'(x)\\,\\textrm d x = f(b) - f(a).\n$$\n\nA similar result, known as **the fundamental theorem for line integrals,** exists for line integrals of conservative vector fields. This theorem can be stated as follows:\n\n> *Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $\\nabla f$ is continuous on $C.$ Then,*\n> \n> \n> $$\n> \\int_{C} \\nabla f \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r = f(\\mathbf r(b)) - f(\\mathbf r(a)).\n> $$\n\nWe'll discuss how this theorem is proved in simple cases at the end of the lesson. But for now, let's take a look at a concrete example of how to apply this theorem." :tutorial/id #uuid "0c3e4762-a85a-5881-81ad-857e0bec5a48" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4306 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "e063a5e6-8819-5814-8c62-dae890a57d71" :step/math-academy-id 26178 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134860}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134824 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133878 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133694 :question/id #uuid "234800a9-ae9c-56c4-8284-84041f129a59" :question/math-academy-id "e-8759"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b643761-8eab-5bd3-a5f5-c85b6ba34a61" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049853} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050983}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134059} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134567} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240906} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240909} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259584} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259585} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259586} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259587}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals Over Parametric Curves"} :step/id #uuid "a10e6c95-833b-528d-a1f9-411415a6a652" :step/math-academy-id 32622 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134850}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134825 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133879 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133695 :question/id #uuid "667923e0-14bc-5bbd-8129-333703cd6616" :question/math-academy-id "e-8760"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e8a1d966-b4a8-5af8-8b10-063cacea6526" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050869}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134213} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134566} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240918} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240919} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240920} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240921} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240924} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240925} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240926} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240928} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240929}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals When the Potential Function Is Not Given"} :step/id #uuid "f869fbb2-2435-5488-b530-01a592efdf62" :step/math-academy-id 32627 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134861}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134850 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133890 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133706 :question/id #uuid "3f724b05-571d-529d-ab2b-db9dd6d2b531" :question/math-academy-id "e-9502"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4519f3b-0fb8-54fd-9e9e-fca3e599b80b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048645} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049633}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134080} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134236} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240905} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240907} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240908} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259588} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259589} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259590} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259591} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259592} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259593} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259594}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals Over Cartesian Curves"} :step/id #uuid "879e7253-5b1c-559d-8414-14057286be07" :step/math-academy-id 35588 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134825}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134860 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135127 :tutorial/content "Let's apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals to evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\nabla f \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r},\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y) = x^2y^3$ and $C$ is the path along the curve $y=x^2$ for $x \\in [0,2].$ \n\nFirst, we must parameterize the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\left\\langle t, \\: t^2\\right\\rangle, \\qquad t \\in [0,2].\n$$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of $C$ are given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(0)\t= \\left\\langle 0, 0\\right\\rangle, \\qquad\n\\mathbf{r}(2)\t= \\left\\langle 2, 4\\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the path's endpoints defined by $C$ are $(0,0)$ and $(2,4).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(2))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(2,4)-f(0,0) \\\\  & ={2}^{2}\\cdot {4}^{3}-{0}^{2}\\cdot {0}^{3} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 64 \\\\  & =256.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice how much faster it is to evaluate a line integral when we can use the fundamental theorem." :tutorial/id #uuid "8ce39e74-c98d-54af-8d93-20f6b5bca39a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5829 :tutorial/title "A Concrete Example"} :step/id #uuid "15f14408-48b8-5f0f-8c55-d5009dc56e57" :step/math-academy-id 35721 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134824}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134861 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135128 :tutorial/content "Here, we prove the fundamental theorem of line integrals in the two-dimensional case. The three-dimensional case is similar.\n\nLet $C$ be a smooth curve $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and suppose that $f(x,y)$ is a differentiable function of two variables such that $\\nabla f$ is continuous on $C.$ We wish to show that\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\nabla f \\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = f(\\mathbf r(b)) - f(\\mathbf r(a)).\n$$\n\nUsing our usual formula for calculating line integrals, we can write the left-hand side as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}∇f\\cdot {r}^{'}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}(\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the chain rule, the integrand above is simply the derivative of $f(\\mathbf r(t))$ with respect to $t.$ Thus, we can write this integral as\n\n$$\n\\int_a^b \\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t}(f(\\mathbf r(t)))\\,\\textrm d t.\n$$\n\nFinally, by the fundamental theorem of calculus, this is equal to \n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf r(b)) - f(\\mathbf r(a)).\n$$\n\nThis concludes the proof in the case where $C$ is smooth. It's easy to show that the theorem also holds for piecewise smooth curves.\n\nFor piecewise smooth curves, let [math]C = C_1\\cup C_2 \\cup \\ldots C_n,[/math] where each [math]C_i[/math] is smooth. Then, we have\n\n\\begin{align*}\n\\require{cancel}\n\\int_C \\nabla f \\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r \n\n&=\n\n\\int_{C_1} \\nabla f \\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r + \\int_{C_2} \\nabla f \\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r + \\cdots + \\int_{C_n} \\nabla f \\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r \\\\[5pt]\n\n& = f(\\mathbf r(a_1)) - f(\\mathbf r(a)) + f(\\mathbf r(a_2)) - f(\\mathbf r(a_1)) + \\cdots + f(\\mathbf r(b)) - f(\\mathbf r(a_{n-1})) \\\\[5pt]\n\n& = \\cancel{f(\\mathbf r(a_1))} - f(\\mathbf r(a)) + \\cancel{f(\\mathbf r(a_2))} - \\cancel{f(\\mathbf r(a_1))} + \\cdots + f(\\mathbf r(b)) - \\cancel{f(\\mathbf r(a_{n-1}))} \\\\[5pt]\n\n&=f(\\mathbf r(b)) - f(\\mathbf r(a)).\n\\end{align*}" :tutorial/id #uuid "0ef0926c-7a69-5fc8-91f0-7f40e3be9be9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5830 :tutorial/title "Proof of the Fundamental Theorem for Smooth Curves"} :step/id #uuid "48691aa9-a37a-52cd-a44c-e9ef2b10ea85" :step/math-academy-id 35722}] :activity/title "The Fundamental Theorem for Line Integrals" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133996}]}
